[Music]  the holiday season  is a time to celebrate and to be around  loved ones  but this year everything from christmas  markets to family gatherings  is having to be scaled back due to the  kovid19 pandemic  social distancing and other restrictions  have left  many feeling less than festive so what  should a good covet christmas look like  this is dw's covid19 special i'm janelle  de malaon  as germany grapples with yet another  record number in coronavirus cases it's  weighing up how best to allow for  merrymaking  while keeping the virus from spreading  and all across europe  it's a similar picture people are  breaking with tradition  as they change their plans to  accommodate restrictions  here in the spanish city of sevilla the  christmas lights that you see behind me  stay off at the moment even at night the  city administration is afraid of big  crowds  that might come and watch them the  general restrictions for christmas here  in spain are not clear yet  but everybody knows this will be a very  different festive season  even in my own family which is partly  german and partly spanish  we don't know what to do yet and if we  will be able to meet each other  at all normally at this time of year the  place behind me would look totally  different  there would be lots of christmas market  stands and the smell of roasted almonds  everywhere to get you in the mood  but what i personally miss the most this  december  is meeting friends in the evening and  drinking mulled wine together  the only thing that cheers me up this  year is mild wine to go  cheers and merry christmas now how to  strike a balance between  necessary interpersonal contact and the  required social distancing  is a big question for many of us as the  christmas holidays get closer  here to help us understand this better  is tegan crews she is an associate  professor and clinical psychologist  at the australian national university  thank you very much for joining us tegan  now according to your research our risk  perception during an encounter with a  person depends on how well we know them  why is it that people find it so hard to  take the necessary coveted precautions  with the people we love  it's something we've been looking at for  a number of years in our research group  and one thing that we've found is that  there's this consistent relationship  where people are much more willing to  take  risks with the people that they are  closest to they're ones that they love  um and that seems to be because we  humans are  hardwired to use that sense of social  connection we have to others  the groups that we belong to and the  communities that we value  we use that as a cue to safety uh it's a  signal that we can trust someone and  that they are a safe person for us to  you know put our life in their hands and  in some ways that makes a lot of sense  you know if you're looking for someone  to take care of your children  your closest networks probably are the  people you can trust the most  on the other hand when it comes to the  spread of a contagious disease  it doesn't make a lot of sense and so i  think that's what we're seeing here is  that our instincts to trust those that  are closest to us are actually  undermining our safety when it comes to  covert 19.  our hard-wired instincts putting putting  us at risk of that can be quite  quite a problem but can you tell us  again how did you come to these  conclusions  yeah we've been conducting a series of  studies over a number of years  um including before covett so we were we  were interested in this  um over the last few years and ran a few  studies looking at risk perception  but we've also looked at it since the  the pandemic began  um and one nice opportunity we had to do  that was a study  that we had running at the beginning of  march we were looking at neighborhoods  and people's connection to their  community  and the original intention was to look  at uh people's sort of uh participation  in community events but um  when the pandemic really gained steam in  march  we changed the the purpose of one  particular project in order to look at  uh how this particular research question  around the link between  our group memberships the trust we have  in others and our risk perception  and what we found was that in our sample  of a couple of hundred people in  australia  um the the stronger that people  identified with their local neighborhood  and their community  um before covert so in early march the  more likely they were to trust their  neighbours  in lockdown which sounds like a great  thing but the problem  in this case with trusting one's  neighbors was that that also predicted  that they were  more comfortable being in close contact  with those neighbors so breaching  physical distancing recommendations for  instance and  spending time in in the physical  presence of their neighbours  felt safer because they had that trust  in their neighbours  so so that would be an example of one of  the studies we've run looking at this  link  where social connection and the sense of  belonging we have with others which is  usually such a protective and positive  thing  can actually undermine our capacity to  respond to a pandemic  of this nature indeed and if social  contact is  needed to stay healthy but at the same  time it puts us at risk  what are we to do then  it is a bit of a catch-22 i think  because i certainly wouldn't want to say  that social connection  isn't important i think it's one of the  most important things when it comes to  protecting our health and indeed our  mental health  over the long term and it's also the  case that people who are  most vulnerable to covert 19  complications are often the same people  who are vulnerable to loneliness  uh to isolation and to the poor health  consequences that we see as a result of  that  um one of the things i think that 2020  has really necessitated for all of us is  to be creative  about how we stay connected to one  another um whether that's through using  virtual means in ways that we might  not have considered prior to this year  or whether it's you know choosing to get  together  outside or in a socially distanced more  safe way of getting together and seeing  our closest contacts i think we need to  find compromises that allow us to stay  connected  while taking as fewer risks as possible  when it comes to covert  you've written that the critical role of  shared identity  and therefore of higher risk taking has  been under recognized in public health  messaging  how exactly should public health  messages change in your opinion  i think that there has perhaps hasn't  been much attention to the to the  difference between  the risks we're willing to take with  strangers on public transport out in our  communities  versus the risks we're willing to take  within our closest networks with our  families and with our friends  and the problem with that is that we're  actually very very ready to accept the  risk of strangers  but much less ready to recognize and  accept the risk in our closest networks  um and in fact that's exactly exactly  where the vast majority of covert  transmission is happening it's happening  in households it's happening  at small gatherings of family and  friends so i think  ideally i'd like to see public health  messaging really be a bit more focused  on how we can  reduce those risks in our closest  networks and how we can celebrate  important milestones like birthdays and  christmas  without putting the people we love in  danger  so social distancing protecting the  people you love thank you very much that  was teagan crewish she is an associate  professor and clinical psychologist  at the australian national university  thank you for your time and insights  today  thanks janelle and for the final time  this week  our science correspondent derek williams  answers one of your questions  for a 20 year old what's the risk of  taking the vaccine compared to not  taking it  most of the questions i'm getting at the  moment involve potential  vaccines and and this one is close to  the top of the list  the experts i've read who discussed this  topic  make several important points first of  all they say  you should look at the numbers that we  have so far  like the data showing that for tens of  thousands of people  in three big vaccine trials there have  been no reported  serious side effects although bear in  mind  that those test subjects didn't have  known  pre-existing health problems for people  who do  there could be more risk involved we  just heard for example about a couple of  cases of people  in the uk who had pre-existing allergies  having a bad reaction to one vaccine but  the experts still say  that the potential risks are pale in  comparison to the risks  involved in catching the virus sure  a 20 year old is a lot less likely to  die from covet 19  than an 80 year old is but but some do  why take that chance if we have  something  that medical authorities say can safely  protect you  which brings me to point two even if a  young person is much less likely  to die from the disease there's also a  chance  that a covet-19 infection could cause  long-term health issues by some  estimates  up to a third of those who who recover  continue to have  more chronic complaints that that  doesn't seem to be age dependent  so so young people have them too  point number three getting vaccinated  also  has a social aspect which is that it  could potentially help protect the  people around you  though that question granted is still up  in the air still  expectations are high that if vaccines  stop people from developing fully  fledged covet 19  they'll also help cut down on  transmission and and that'll slow down  the pandemic  for all of us um finally don't forget  that young people who aren't in  essential jobs will be pretty  far down on the list for vaccination in  most countries  by the time most of them can get a shot  we're going to have a lot more  information about  any potential serious side effects but  based on what we know now  most experts expect those to be very  [Music]  rare  you

